From 395c6408ad68794e4f668f87e15c8b2a59ae56fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CreateCVOnline <dev@createcvonline.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:19:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Give the CV document its own language, separate from the
 interface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A French user signing up on /fr got a CV headed "Work Experience",
"Education", "Skills" — and that is what printed in the PDF they sent to
employers. The French and German headings had existed since the localised
marketing pages shipped; they were wired to the previews and to nothing
else.

Language belongs to the document, not the account, because someone applying
in Paris and Berlin needs a French CV and a German Lebenslauf side by side.
So CVData gains `language`, defaulting to English so every document written
before the field parses unchanged, and the user profile's long-dead `locale`
field becomes the interface language it was always meant to be.

Localised with it, because a half-translated document is worse than an
English one:

- Section headings on new CVs, and on switching a CV's language. Switching
  never overwrites a heading the user renamed — a heading counts as
  untouched only when it matches that section's default in some language.
- Dates. `Mar 2021 – Present` / `mars 2021 – aujourd'hui` / `März 2021 –
  heute`. Month names are written out rather than taken from Intl: ICU data
  differs between local Chromium, Vercel and Lambda, and a CV re-downloaded
  a year later has to come out byte-identical.
- The `Your Name` placeholder, which 59 of the 61 templates carried inline
  and which is the largest text on an empty page.
- The cover letter — salutation, sign-off, subject line and date. These are
  conventions rather than translations: French closes with "Je vous prie
  d'agréer…", German with "Mit freundlichen Grüßen", and the English
  sincerely/faithfully rule only applies to English.
- Europass and Government, the two templates that print field labels. The
  CEFR grid uses the Commission's own headings.
- The multi-page PDF footer: "Page 1 of 2" / "1 sur 2" / "Seite 1 von 2".
- `lang` on the document root, which decides hyphenation and is what makes
  the exported PDF reach PDF/UA.

`SECTION_META.defaultLabel` is gone. It was a second copy of the same
thirteen strings and had already drifted by one word — the test written to
assert the two agreed failed on its first run, which is the argument.

1,626 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
